# Reelist — Cursor rules

You are working on Reelist, a social movie watched-list tracker (Letterboxd-style).
Read architecture.md and design.md before generating any non-trivial code — they
are the source of truth for structure and visual system. This file is the
day-to-day ruleset for how to write code in this repo.

## Stack (do not deviate without asking)

- Next.js (App Router), React, TypeScript
- Supabase: Postgres, Auth, Row-Level Security, Storage (not used yet — no
  user-uploaded images in scope)
- TMDB API for all movie/TV metadata and imagery
- Deployed on Vercel, installable as a PWA
- No native mobile framework (no Flutter, no React Native) — the PWA is the
  mobile app for now. Do not suggest introducing one.

## Non-negotiables

- Never expose the TMDB API key or Supabase service-role key in client code.
  All TMDB calls go through a Next.js API route (`/app/api/tmdb/...`).
- Every table that holds user data needs an RLS policy before it ships —
  no table goes live with RLS disabled "temporarily."
- No feature outside the current phase in phases.md gets built early, even if
  it looks like a quick add. Flag it and move on.
- IMDb rating is a static "Coming soon" label. Do not wire up OMDb or any
  other API for it — this was an explicit decision, not an oversight.
- No cast-to-IMDb redirect links. Cast list is TMDB name + photo + role only.
- No user-uploaded movie logos. If TMDB has no logo for a title, render the
  empty/placeholder state — do not add an upload flow.
- Movies-only in the data model until phases.md says otherwise. Don't
  preemptively build season/episode tracking.

## Code style

- TypeScript strict mode. No `any` unless there's a comment explaining why.
- Functional React components, hooks over classes.
- Co-locate a component's styles/types with the component unless they're
  shared across routes.
- Server Components by default; add `"use client"` only where interactivity
  actually requires it (forms, buttons with handlers, anything using state).
- Data fetching in Server Components or API routes, not client-side `useEffect`
  fetches, unless it's genuinely a live/interactive case.
- Prefer small, composable functions over one large handler. Name functions
  for what they do, not how they're implemented.
- Comments explain *why*, not *what* — don't narrate obvious code.

## Naming

- Files: kebab-case (`add-movie-form.tsx`)
- Components: PascalCase
- DB tables and columns: snake_case, matching architecture.md's schema
  exactly — don't invent new column names ad hoc

## Styling

- Tailwind CSS, tokens driven by the palette and type scale in design.md.
  Don't introduce raw hex values inline — extend the Tailwind theme instead.
- Respect the light/dark mode toggle everywhere — never hardcode a color
  that only works in one mode.
- Glass panels use the `.glass` utility (backdrop-blur + translucent
  surface) defined in design.md — don't hand-roll a one-off blur effect
  per component.

## When generating code, always

1. Check whether the feature belongs in the current phase (phases.md) before
   writing it.
2. Check RLS implications before adding a new query — ask "can a logged-out
   user, or another user, see this by accident?"
3. Keep TMDB response shaping in one place (a `lib/tmdb.ts` mapper), not
   duplicated per component.
4. Flag anything that would cost money (Supabase overage, a paid API tier)
   instead of silently adding it.

## When unsure

Ask, don't guess — especially on schema changes, RLS policies, or anything
touching auth. A wrong guess here is expensive to unwind later.
